# /app/<username> is a route, not a file. Status 200 is a rewrite, so the URL stays put --
# which is what boot.js reads to decide what to render.
#
# WHY THIS FILE NEEDS _routes.json TO WORK
# ----------------------------------------
# Cloudflare Pages: "Redirects defined in the _redirects file are not applied to requests
# served by Pages Functions, even if the Function route matches the URL pattern. If your
# Pages application uses Functions, you must migrate any behaviors from the _redirects file
# to the code in the appropriate /functions route, or exclude the route from Functions."
#
# TraxWax has functions/ (the Discogs proxy), so by default this whole file was inert --
# measured: the rules parsed, /_redirects was consumed as a config file rather than served,
# and /app/lanebecker still fell through to the SPA fallback (the landing page).
#
# public/_routes.json now pins Functions to /api/* ONLY. Everything else is served by the
# static asset store, where these rules DO apply. Keep the two files in sync: if a Function
# is ever added outside /api/, add its prefix to _routes.json "include" and move any
# affected routing here into that Function.
#
# No executable asset may live under /app/ -- Pages follows redirects "regardless of whether
# or not an asset matches the incoming request", so a script parked there would be served as
# HTML and rejected by the module MIME check. boot.js and app.js sit at the public/ root.
# DESTINATION IS /app/ (trailing slash), NOT /app/index.html. Pages pretty-URL
# normalization 308s /app/index.html away, and a rewrite targeting that hidden path was
# measured inert (2026-08-28: /app/lanebecker fell through to the SPA fallback while this
# file was provably deployed and syntactically valid). /app/ is the URL that actually 200s.
/app      /app/  200
/app/*    /app/  200

